From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Sat, 15 Oct 1994 22:22:12 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-254] The Procreation Instinct

Like David Friedman, I, too, think that there is more of a genetic impetus to
procreate than Nick Szabo said in "The Procreation Instinct Myth."  The most
obvious evidence of this is the psychological phenomenon known as "Baby Hunger,"
which manifests primarily in human females:

"Lois Davitz, author of _Baby_Hunger_, studied the desire to have a baby among
200 women drawn from a cross-section of social classes, ethnic groups and
religious backgrounds, and ranging in age from 18 to 40-plus.  Sixty-nine percent
of them were childless.  Her investigations led her to the conclusions that

	"every woman experiences baby hunger - the quickening, bubbling, thriving
	driving desire for a child.  The urge may come at twenty, thirty, or in some
	cases close to forty and even beyond.  But no matter the age or the frequency,
	when baby hunger erupts there is no escape from the all-consuming feeling...
	when a woman has baby hunger, nothing else is as important to her as this
	innder drive to have a child.

"Davitz found that baby hunger is most likely to strike childless women in their
late twenties and middle thirties.  For some the desire is triggered by external
stimuli, such as holding a kitten or being hugged by a child.  For others the feeling
seems to arise from within.

"Whatever the cause, once in this state women see babies everywhere, in the streets,
in supermarkets, on TV and in magazines.  They often start eating better, cutting
down on smoking and having medical check-ups.  They may knit booties for friends or
start collecting baby gear.  One woman described buying a cradle at a garage sale
and hiding it in her basement.

"More than half of women with baby hunger experience physical signals that imitate
pregnancy.  They suffer from swollen breasts, lower back pains, enlarged stomachs
and menstrual dysfunction.  Most also experience a heightened emotional sensitivity
as well as heightened sexual responsiveness.  They become seductive and encourage or
initiate sex, but intimacy and sexual pleasure take second place to the desire to be
pregnant.

"Women with baby hunger have dreams about buying or stealing babies, about being
pregnant and cuddling babies.  Those who are unable to fall pregnant feel depressed
and empty.  Baby stealing is one of the very few crimes that only women commit.

"Baby hunger is not selective.  It attacks women who are active in the fight against
overpopulation, women who have always been vehemently opposed to having children and
who may have had abortions in the past.  They suddenly change.  One such woman said:

	"I'd crawl out of bed, sit in the kitchen, have cigarette after cigarette
	trying to remember what made me so sure before that I didn't want a child.
	Nothing worked... None of my old excuses held up any more... All I could
	think about was getting pregnant... I faced the fact that some things in life
	are emotional - having nothing to do with logical arguments.  It's like
	falling in love. (Davitz, pp88-89)

"Single women start assessing dates as fathering material and many are propelled
into marriage by baby hunger.  In their late thirties some single women even choose
to get pregnant by virtual strangers or by artificial insemination.

"It comes as a shock to husbands when their wives experience baby hunger, especially
if they have previously been against having children.  The men are bewildered by
their wives' desperation and annoyed and embarrassed by their obsessiveness.  In the
...[oops. typo]...  Some men refuse to comply with their wives' wishes.  One of the
respondents in the Davitz study left her husband when he refused to have a vasectomy
reversal, even though she still loved him and didn't want anyone else.

"Men may be interested in children and want to have them, but they never experience
anything like the absorbing, obsessive, profound feelings of baby hunger.  Davitz
says:

	"In general my results indicate that men _do_not_ and _cannot_ feel the
	powerful biologically-based emotional urge of baby hunger...  Some very much
	want to become fathers, yet they never consider staying home and reevaluating
	their childless state.  Men do not retreat to the washroom and weep uncontrol-
	lably because their wives are reluctant to bear children.  No man said he law
	awake, sleepless, with fantasies of cuddling, nuzzling babies at his breast.

"Men tend to think in terms of children, not babies.  They imagine playing ball or
teaching their kids to ride a bike.  Women fantasize about an infant snuggling in
their arms, about hugging it and squeezing it, and about its tininess and helplessness.

"Men often want to put off having a baby until their financial position improves.  For
a woman with baby hunger, the size of the bank balance seems irrelevant..."

	- Frances Kendall, "The SeX-Y Factor: Gender Differences at Home and Work,"
	pp. 92-94

Kendall also cites evidence to the effect that this is not the result of societal
conditioning.  Nick seems to be right as far as the male of the species goes: we have
no strong procreative instinct.  But the female has a very, very strong one indeed.
And, as Kendall points out lastly in this chapter, those women who don't have any
such instinct will soon select themselves out of the gene pool given access to
effective contraception.

