From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sun, 16 Oct 1994 01:49:54 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-256] The Procreation Instinct

My thanks to Tim Starr for his interesting excerpts from
_Baby Hunger_.  He adds:

> such instinct will soon select themselves out of the gene pool 
>given access to  effective contraception.

Is there evidence that these emotions do in fact make women less 
likely to use contraception?  There is no instictual connection 
between attraction to babies and the activity of discontuing birth
control -- making that connection requires abstract,
conscious decision making.  There is no short term satiation
from removing the birth control from which the emotional
mind could connect removing the birth control with having
a baby, in the way there is short term satation of chocolate
craving from buying chocolate, of sleepiness from laying
down on a comfy bed, etc.  There is no chance for the
behavioral learning that dominates at the emotional level to
kick in and turn craving for babies into a decision to remove
birth control.  If the emotional mind doesn't make that connection, 
then abstract decision-making cognition, dominated by memes, has 
no difficulty in overriding the "hunger".

How much variation in baby hunger is there betweeen women
from which evolution could selet?  Is there evidence that 
"hungry" women in fact have more children than women who 
do not exhibit these emotions?

Is there evidence that these emotions cannot be satiated
by pets, dolls, or adopted children?  There is much
more chance of emotional connection between act and satiation 
when one doesn't have to wait nine months.

Assuming the author's studies have been independtly repeated
and verified, it may be more accurate to describe this as a
"child care instinct" triggered by the site of babies, rather 
than hunger to have a baby.  In any case, it seems quite
prone to memetic short-circuit, as the demographic statistics
of populations with high access to and ability to use birth
control dramatically show.  Memes that influence the decision
in favor of birth control have and will evolve far faster than 
the genes for mothering instinct can evolve into genes for baby 
hunger.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

