From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Sat, 15 Oct 1994 10:22:36 GMT
Subject: [#94-10-257] SCI: Many-Worlds FAQ

I agree with Nick Szabo that there is more to probabilities and
randomness than the ratios of the various probabilitistic outcomes, but
this is a very complex area which I have sought to avoid.  In seeking
to demonstrate that an unlimited sequence of heads and tails is random
we have to apply further checks (probably an infinite number of them)
to establish that it couldn't be encoded in compressed form.  I am,
however, content with showing that the correct ratios are reached in the
infinite limit, in many-worlds, and leave it at that.

I couldn't see the point Nick was making between EPR, FTL and
randomness, if one was intended.  Certainly many-worlds does not imply
any FTL effects, since everything is ultimately derivable from what is
assumed to be set of local (slower-than-light) physical laws.

Michael Price                        price@price.demon.co.uk

