control system is just one result. Then there's neural net based OCRs,
etc. Yeah, they aren't androids, but progress is slow.

> >From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
> >Tim Starr writes:
>>> Ludwig von Mises is the right one.  Just as socialists went hysterical when
>>> Mises proved the impossibility of rational economic calculation under pure
>>> socialism, so Searle proved the impossibility of what he calls "strong AI."
>>
>>  But Searle didn't prove this. Searle made an argument, which many
>>people think is irrelevent or incorrect.
>
>"But Mises didn't prove that.  Mises made an argument, which many people think
> is irrelevant or incorrect."

   This statement would make sense if I accepted the analogy between Searle
and Mises, I don't. Furthermore, I think Mises's "proof" isn't flawless. 
However, what Mises was arguing against, and what Searle is, are two
completely different phenomena, and one is measurable.

> Searle pointed out the irrefutable fact that mere syntactic operations per-
> formers lack a key ingredient of the mind: semantic capability.  No one has
> proven him wrong about this, they've simply proposed to either build machines
> which can also simulate semantic operations as well as syntactical ones or
> they've declared semantic operations to be irrelevant.
 
   Those are some pretty big assertions. Everytime I see the statement
"the mind can do X", I must raise my eyebrow. Searle, Penrose, and others
claim properties of the mind that are arguable, such as the ability
to "truly" understand the meaning of a word, or to "intuit" a math
theorem, rather like Plato thought one could truly understand "beauty"
vs what they claim computers will only be able to do: only be able to
know what a word is in terms of some rule-set which connects it to
other words. 
   However, this is not the only way to do semantics. One could, for example,
build a neural net trained to recognize the image of an apple. Then, the
computer program would understand the semantic content of apple to mean
"appleness", or what its neural net recognized as such. Then, one could
create the category of fruits, and include in it apple.  This combination
of heuristic "symbol manipulation" and neural-net sensory manipulation
would understand words in relation to other words, and their relation
to the real world.

   Of course, Searle would claim that the neural net "didn't really
understand appleness", but that's like saying your eyes "really 
understand a chair". It is not the functioning of the piece, but
the functioning of the whole, which leads to understanding.

   Searle's Chinese Room argument proves that humans don't understand
language, for the neurons by themselves don't understand the meaning
of the things they are processing, such as the true meaning of
libertarianism.

> >...After all, just what is the
> >objective definition of "consciousness" and "intelligence" How can
> >a philosophical argument disprove anything about a system which we
> >know nothing about?
> 
> "After all, just what is the definition of 'socialism' and 'calculation'?  How
